&lt;h1&gt;


Risk Management: Security Controls, Compliance, and Business Continuity
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cybersecurity is not simply about preventing hackers from accessing computer systems. Organizations must also understand the risks that threaten their operations, sensitive information, financial resources, and reputation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Risk management provides a structured approach to identifying potential threats, evaluating their impact, and implementing controls to reduce exposure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An effective cybersecurity risk management program helps organizations make informed security decisions, prioritize investments, and maintain business operations even when incidents occur.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What Is Cybersecurity Risk Management?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cybersecurity risk management is the process of identifying, assessing, treating, and monitoring risks associated with information systems and organizational assets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A risk generally involves the possibility that a threat will exploit a vulnerability and cause harm to an organization.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an organization may operate an internet-facing server that contains an unpatched vulnerability. A threat actor could exploit that weakness to gain unauthorized access, potentially exposing sensitive customer information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Risk management helps organizations understand these scenarios and determine what actions should be taken to reduce their likelihood or impact.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The primary objectives include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Identifying critical assets.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Understanding potential threats and vulnerabilities.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Evaluating likelihood and business impact.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Selecting appropriate security controls.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Monitoring changes in risk.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Maintaining acceptable levels of organizational risk.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Risk management should be an ongoing process because technology, threats, business operations, and regulatory requirements continuously change.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Understanding Risk Components
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Several concepts are important when evaluating cybersecurity risk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Asset:&lt;/strong&gt; Something valuable to an organization, such as customer information, servers, applications, or intellectual property.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Threat:&lt;/strong&gt; Something capable of causing harm, such as a cybercriminal, malicious insider, natural disaster, or malware infection.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Vulnerability:&lt;/strong&gt; A weakness that could be exploited by a threat.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Exploit:&lt;/strong&gt; A method or technique used to take advantage of a vulnerability.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Impact:&lt;/strong&gt; The potential damage resulting from a successful attack or other adverse event.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Likelihood:&lt;/strong&gt; The probability that a particular event will occur.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, consider a database containing sensitive customer information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The database is an asset. An external attacker is a potential threat. An unpatched vulnerability is a weakness. Exploiting that vulnerability could result in unauthorized access and a data breach.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Understanding these relationships helps security teams evaluate which risks require the most attention.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Risk Assessment
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Risk assessment is the process of identifying and evaluating potential risks to an organization's systems and operations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Organizations commonly use qualitative and quantitative assessment methods.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Qualitative Risk Assessment
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Qualitative assessments use descriptive categories to evaluate risk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an organization might classify risks as:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Low.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Medium.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;High.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Critical.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These classifications are based on factors such as likelihood, potential impact, and existing security controls.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A risk matrix can help visualize the relationship between likelihood and impact.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, a highly likely event with a severe business impact would generally receive a high-risk classification.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Qualitative assessments are useful when exact financial estimates are unavailable or when organizations need a relatively straightforward prioritization method.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Quantitative Risk Assessment
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Quantitative risk assessment uses numerical values to estimate potential financial losses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One commonly used approach involves the following calculations:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Single Loss Expectancy (SLE):&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SLE = Asset Value × Exposure Factor&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The exposure factor represents the percentage of an asset's value that could be lost during a particular incident.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Annualized Rate of Occurrence (ARO):&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ARO estimates how frequently a particular event is expected to occur each year.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Annualized Loss Expectancy (ALE):&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;ALE = SLE × ARO&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, assume a business application has an estimated value of $100,000. A successful incident could result in a 40% loss of that value.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The SLE would be:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;$100,000 × 0.40 = $40,000&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the organization estimates that the event could occur twice per year, the ARO would be 2.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The ALE would therefore be:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;$40,000 × 2 = $80,000&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This calculation estimates an annual expected loss of $80,000.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Quantitative methods can help organizations compare security investments with potential financial losses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, these estimates depend on assumptions and should not be treated as exact predictions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Risk Treatment Strategies
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After identifying and evaluating risks, organizations must decide how to handle them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are four commonly recognized risk treatment strategies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Risk Mitigation
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Risk mitigation involves implementing controls to reduce the likelihood or impact of a risk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an organization may deploy multifactor authentication, patch vulnerable systems, encrypt sensitive information, and improve network segmentation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These controls do not necessarily eliminate risk, but they can reduce it to an acceptable level.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Risk Acceptance
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Risk acceptance occurs when an organization knowingly decides to tolerate a particular risk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This may happen when the potential impact is relatively low or when the cost of implementing additional controls exceeds the expected benefit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Accepted risks should be documented, reviewed, and approved by the appropriate stakeholders.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Risk Transfer
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Risk transfer involves shifting some financial consequences of a risk to another party.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cybersecurity insurance is a common example.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An organization may purchase insurance to help cover certain costs associated with a data breach or cyberattack.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, insurance does not eliminate the underlying security risk, and coverage depends on the policy's terms and exclusions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Risk Avoidance
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Risk avoidance involves eliminating an activity that creates unacceptable risk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an organization may decide not to deploy an application that cannot meet its security requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Avoidance is appropriate when the potential risk is too significant to justify continuing the activity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Security Controls
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Security controls are safeguards designed to protect organizational assets and reduce cybersecurity risk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Controls can be classified by their purpose and how they operate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Control Categories by Function
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Preventive controls:&lt;/strong&gt; Attempt to stop incidents before they occur.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Examples include firewalls, access controls, encryption, and multifactor authentication.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Detective controls:&lt;/strong&gt; Identify suspicious activity or security violations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Examples include intrusion detection systems, security monitoring, and audit logs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Corrective controls:&lt;/strong&gt; Help restore systems or correct security weaknesses after an incident.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Examples include patching, malware removal, and restoring systems from backups.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Deterrent controls:&lt;/strong&gt; Discourage individuals from attempting unauthorized activities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Examples include warning banners, security policies, and visible surveillance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Compensating controls:&lt;/strong&gt; Provide an alternative safeguard when the preferred control cannot be implemented.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, if an older application cannot support modern authentication, an organization may isolate it behind a secure gateway and restrict access to approved systems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Control Categories by Implementation
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Security controls can also be categorized based on how they are implemented.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Technical controls:&lt;/strong&gt; Firewalls, encryption, antivirus, and access management systems.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Administrative controls:&lt;/strong&gt; Security policies, procedures, risk assessments, and employee training.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Physical controls:&lt;/strong&gt; Locks, security guards, cameras, and access badges.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A mature security program uses multiple control types to provide defense in depth.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Compliance and Security Frameworks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Organizations often need to meet regulatory requirements, contractual obligations, and internal security standards.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Compliance helps ensure that appropriate safeguards are implemented and that organizations can demonstrate adherence to applicable requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Several widely used frameworks and standards support cybersecurity risk management.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF):&lt;/strong&gt; Provides guidance for managing cybersecurity risks through the functions Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, and Recover.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ISO/IEC 27001:&lt;/strong&gt; Defines requirements for establishing, implementing, maintaining, and continually improving an Information Security Management System.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;PCI DSS:&lt;/strong&gt; Establishes security requirements for organizations that store, process, or transmit payment card information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;HIPAA:&lt;/strong&gt; Establishes requirements for protecting certain health information handled by covered entities and business associates in the United States.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;SOC 2:&lt;/strong&gt; Provides an independent examination and reporting framework concerning controls relevant to specified trust services criteria.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Organizations should understand which requirements apply to their operations rather than assuming that every framework is mandatory for every business.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Compliance is also not the same as complete security. An organization may satisfy a particular standard and still have vulnerabilities that require attention.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Business Continuity and Disaster Recovery
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Risk management must also consider events that interrupt normal business operations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Business Continuity Planning (BCP) focuses on maintaining essential business functions during and after disruptive events.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Disaster Recovery (DR) focuses more specifically on restoring IT systems, applications, and data following a disruption.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, if ransomware makes an organization's production systems unavailable, its business continuity plan may identify alternative processes for maintaining critical operations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Its disaster recovery plan may define how to restore servers, applications, and data from verified backups.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two important recovery metrics are:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Recovery Time Objective (RTO):&lt;/strong&gt; The maximum targeted amount of time a system or business process can remain unavailable before recovery.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Recovery Point Objective (RPO):&lt;/strong&gt; The maximum targeted amount of data loss measured in time that an organization can tolerate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, an RTO of four hours means the organization aims to restore the affected service within four hours.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An RPO of one hour means the organization aims to limit data loss to no more than approximately one hour of updates.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These objectives help organizations select appropriate backup schedules, recovery technologies, and infrastructure.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Third-Party and Supply Chain Risk
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Organizations frequently rely on external vendors, cloud providers, software developers, and service providers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These relationships can introduce cybersecurity risks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, a company may have strong internal security controls but depend on a third-party provider that stores sensitive customer information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If that provider experiences a data breach, the company may still face operational, financial, legal, and reputational consequences.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Third-party risk management involves evaluating vendors before establishing relationships and monitoring their security practices over time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Important activities include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Reviewing vendor security policies.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Evaluating independent security assessments.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Assessing data handling and access requirements.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Reviewing incident notification procedures.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Including security requirements in contracts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Monitoring&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hari Kamis, dua jam sebelum rilis, dan Dari satu-satunya orang yang tahu langkah cutover mana saja yang sudah selesai. Checklist-nya ada di satu tab spreadsheet, runbook-nya di shared drive, dan diskusinya di thread chat yang tidak bisa ditemukan siapa pun. Yang sebenarnya dibutuhkan tim adalah aplikasi mungil dengan empat checkbox dan dua penanggung jawab, tepat di sebelah file-filenya. Menulisnya gampang. Meng-host-nya, memberinya database, menyimpan key-nya dengan aman, dan memberinya URL, bagian itulah yang biasanya macet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Apa itu kategori Custom App
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://osfoundry.io/id" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;osFoundry&lt;/a&gt; mengumpulkan semua yang dibutuhkan untuk membuat dan menjalankan aplikasi kecil buatan sendiri dalam satu kategori di sidebar aplikasi desktop, bernama CUSTOM APP. Isinya lima layar. Room adalah ruang bersama tempat aplikasi tinggal sebagai tab. App Code adalah repository di balik sebuah aplikasi. Deployed Apps adalah daftar, untuk seluruh workspace, berisi apa saja yang sedang live. Snippets mengumpulkan aplikasi mungil yang tidak pernah butuh repository. Domains mengelola alamat web tempat aplikasi-aplikasi itu disajikan. Kalau kamu kenal Notion, bayangkan sebuah halaman yang tab-tabnya berisi aplikasi yang berjalan, bukan dokumen, dengan lapisan hosting kecil ala Vercel di bawahnya: setiap aplikasi punya database, secret, scheduled job, dan subdomain sendiri, seperti dijelaskan di &lt;a href="https://osfoundry.io/id/features/internal-apps" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;halaman fitur aplikasi internal&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Sekilas layar build
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fgn8mxrevrdmyt1lec0nf.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fgn8mxrevrdmyt1lec0nf.jpg" alt="Tampilan build di aplikasi desktop osFoundry dengan daftar Pages, preview kosong, kotak deskripsi dengan tombol Build my site, dan panel chat Maestro" width="800" height="415"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cara tercepat melihat bagaimana sebuah aplikasi dimulai adalah lewat tampilan build di aplikasi desktop. Tangkapan layar ini dari builder Websites (contoh proyek "Hearth Bakery"), yang memakai alur deskripsikan-lalu-build yang sama dengan yang ditawarkan Room lewat kotak "Describe the app you want".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pages, di kiri: bagian aplikasi yang sudah ada, dengan Add page di bawahnya.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Preview, di tengah: apa yang sedang kamu bangun, dengan ukuran desktop, tablet, dan ponsel di atasnya. Label "On this computer" menandakan belum ada yang dipublikasikan.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Kotak deskripsi: kamu tulis apa yang dilakukan aplikasinya, untuk siapa, dan kesan seperti apa yang diinginkan, lalu tekan Build my site. Maestro, asisten AI aplikasi ini, yang menulis file-filenya.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Panel Maestro, di kanan: percakapan yang membangun dan mengubah aplikasi. Use a folder mengarahkannya ke materimu sendiri, bukan membiarkannya menebak-nebak.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Publish needs credits, di atas: build dan preview berjalan di komputermu; hosting adalah langkah yang memakai kredit workspace.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Di dalam Room, idenya sama dengan susunan yang berbeda: deretan tab aplikasi di atas, aplikasi yang dipilih di tengah, dan panel kanan dengan dua tab, Maestro dan Details.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Use case 1: tim rilis menaruh checklist di tempat pekerjaannya berada
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Siapa: seorang release manager dan tiga rekan timnya yang sedang menyiapkan peluncuran.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Situasinya: langkah cutover, runbook, dan release tracker tersebar di tiga tool, dan di hari rilis tidak ada yang percaya pada spreadsheet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yang mereka lakukan di osFoundry:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Membuat room bernama "Launch Ops" lewat New Room, lalu menambahkan release tracker dan file-file peluncuran sebagai tab.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Menekan tombol + di deretan tab. Pemilih New Tab menampilkan aplikasi yang terpasang dan sebuah katalog (Incident log, Retro board, On-call rota, Changelog builder), yang bisa difilter dengan All, Forked, atau Snippets.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Di "Describe the app you want", klik Build a snippet dan minta checklist cutover dengan satu penanggung jawab per item. Snippet adalah mini-app yang bisa dijalankan dan terdiri dari beberapa file. Snippet tidak punya repository, versi, maupun deploy, dan langsung berjalan di dalam tab.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Tetap mengobrol dengan Maestro di panel kanan selama checklist terbuka, dan memakai tab Details untuk melihat room ini tentang apa dan apa saja yang sudah terjadi di dalamnya.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hasil akhirnya: satu room tempat checklist (di demo: Freeze the 2.4 branch, Run the order-index migration, Smoke-test the order desk, Post the release note) berada di sebelah runbook dan tracker. Nantinya, layar Snippets menampilkan semua snippet dari semua room. Dari sana seseorang bisa meng-Export satu snippet sebagai JSON untuk workspace lain, atau mem-Promote-nya menjadi repository sungguhan ketika sudah terlalu besar untuk sebuah tab.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Use case 2: developer memberi tool internal backend yang sungguhan
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Siapa: seorang developer solo yang membuat tool pendaftaran dan pesanan untuk tim support.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Situasinya: versi pertama berjalan di laptop dengan file SQLite. Sekarang tiga tim membutuhkannya, tool ini harus memanggil API pembayaran, dan pembersihan setiap malam harus jalan tanpa ada yang perlu ingat menekan tombol.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F4wy18hiqj2jz9u8ta06y.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F4wy18hiqj2jz9u8ta06y.jpg" alt="Tab Database di IDE osFoundry yang menampilkan data/app.db dengan tabel migrations, sessions, dan users, serta baris-baris users dalam grid read-only" width="800" height="310"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yang dia lakukan di osFoundry:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Selagi aplikasi masih berjalan lokal, buka proyeknya di IDE dan klik tab Database. Tab ini menemukan file &lt;code&gt;.db&lt;/code&gt; dan &lt;code&gt;.sqlite&lt;/code&gt; milik aplikasi dan membuka tabel dalam mode read-only, 200 baris per halaman (contoh data di atas), supaya kamu bisa melihat apa yang benar-benar disimpan aplikasi sebelum memindahkan apa pun.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Memindahkan aplikasi ke room bersama. Dialog Move to cloud menyalin aplikasi ke Cloud room supaya semua anggotanya bisa memakainya, dan melaporkan tabel mana yang disalin dan mana yang tetap di perangkat. Aplikasi dan datanya juga tetap ada di perangkat ini.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Membuka pengaturan tab aplikasi. Di bagian Secrets, App Runtime Secrets menampilkan key yang dideklarasikan aplikasi; Add a custom secret menyimpan yang baru, dan Rotate mengganti sebuah nilai. Aplikasi lalu menampilkan "Secrets updated. Redeploy to apply changes."&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Mendeklarasikan pembersihan itu di bawah &lt;code&gt;container.cron&lt;/code&gt; dalam manifest aplikasi, lalu membuka Scheduled jobs dan klik Sync from manifest. Setiap job bisa di-pause, dilanjutkan, atau dijalankan sekali dengan Run now.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hasil akhirnya: aplikasi dengan database Postgres sendiri. Di osFoundry setiap Room App mendapat satu, dengan pgvector dan TimescaleDB terpasang, dan connection string-nya datang sebagai secret saat runtime (&lt;a href="https://osfoundry.io/id/features/per-app-postgres" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Postgres per aplikasi&lt;/a&gt;). Database browser-nya punya editor baris dengan filter dan tampilan SQL khusus SELECT, dan Database snapshots menyediakan New snapshot, Restore, serta link download. Perubahan kode dilakukan di App Code, tempat developer bisa mengerjakan versi seperti version/2.4 tanpa menyentuh main, mengecek Manifest (entry point, visibilitas, connector yang dibutuhkan), dan mengikuti pohon Versions sebelum rilis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Use case 3: pemilik toko memublikasikan halaman dan terus memantaunya
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Siapa: seseorang yang menjalankan toko roti kecil dan tidak menulis kode.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Situasinya: dia ingin situs sederhana berisi menu dan jam buka, di alamatnya sendiri, dan ingin tahu berapa biaya agar situs itu tetap online.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F6z1x0j0blwfzw0or1bkc.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F6z1x0j0blwfzw0or1bkc.jpg" alt="Builder Websites di osFoundry yang menampilkan situs toko roti tiga halaman versi pertama di preview, dan balasan Maestro bahwa teksnya masih tebakan sampai pemilik membagikan menu dan foto" width="800" height="415"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yang dia lakukan di osFoundry:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Membuka Websites, menekan New Website, mendeskripsikan toko roti, lalu menekan Build my site. Versi pertama (di atas, contoh) memakai teks placeholder, dan Maestro mengatakannya dengan terus terang.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Menekan Use a folder dan memilih folder berisi menu asli dan foto, lalu meminta perubahan dengan bahasa biasa, misalnya "Make the headline bigger."&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Kalau sudah pas, publikasikan. Hosting memakai kredit workspace. Who can open it menawarkan Workspace members only, Anyone with the link, atau login milik aplikasi itu sendiri; situs toko roti publik memakai Anyone with the link.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Di Domains, mendaftarkan domain atau menambahkan domain yang sudah dimiliki, lalu mengedit record DNS-nya. Events menunjukkan siapa mengubah record yang mana, dan Settings berisi perpanjangan, privasi, dan kode transfer.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F93mdy08e44jtz1itp23e.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F93mdy08e44jtz1itp23e.jpg" alt="Contoh situs toko roti yang sudah jadi di preview osFoundry, dengan headline besar di atas foto roti dan Maestro yang mengonfirmasi perubahan headline" width="800" height="415"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hasil akhirnya: situs yang memakai teks dan fotonya sendiri, disajikan di domainnya sendiri. Deployed Apps kemudian menjawab pertanyaan yang benar-benar ditanyakan seorang pemilik. Apps menampilkan setiap aplikasi dan di mana ia disajikan, Activity menampilkan apa yang berubah, Usage menampilkan biaya build dan storage per aplikasi, dan Previews menampilkan versi preview yang masih berjalan.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Bagaimana ia terhubung dengan bagian lain osFoundry
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Custom App jarang bekerja sendirian. Maestro adalah asisten di panel kanan setiap Room dan di tampilan build; ia menulis dan mengedit kode bersamamu. IDE menyediakan tab Database, source control, dan editor untuk aplikasi yang berawal dari folder di komputermu. Websites adalah jalur yang lebih cepat kalau "aplikasi"-nya sebenarnya situs publik. Snippets hanya terhubung ke App Code lewat Promote, jadi tool sekali pakai bisa menjadi repository yang dirawat tanpa perlu ditulis ulang. Pekerjaan terjadwal di dalam aplikasi ditangani oleh cron job aplikasi, yang dijelaskan di &lt;a href="https://osfoundry.io/id/platform/app-cron" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;halaman platform app cron&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Pertanyaan yang sering diajukan pengguna baru
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Apakah Room itu ruang chat?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Bukan. Inti sebuah Room adalah tab-tab aplikasinya. Chat Maestro ada di panel pendamping di sebelah kanan, di samping Details room tersebut.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Kapan sebaiknya membuat snippet, bukan aplikasi?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Ketika tool-nya hanya beberapa file dan cukup berjalan di dalam satu room, misalnya formatter tanggal atau pengecekan webhook. Menyimpan snippet tidak memasang apa pun dan tidak men-deploy apa pun. Kalau nanti butuh versi, secret, atau database, Promote snippet itu menjadi repository.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Bisakah dua aplikasi berbagi satu database?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Setiap aplikasi punya Postgres sendiri. Berbagi dilakukan lewat izin eksplisit: tab Database di room menampilkan Incoming Requests, Active Grants, dan Outgoing Access, dan setiap permintaan bisa disetujui atau ditolak.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Apakah mencobanya berbayar?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Build dan preview di komputermu tidak butuh kredit. Publikasi, deploy, dan pendaftaran domain memakai kredit workspace. Kamu bisa mengunduh aplikasi desktopnya di &lt;a href="https://osfoundry.io/id/download" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;osfoundry.io/id/download&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Sell a Minecraft plugin, a FiveM script, a Discord bot or a desktop app, and someone will use it without paying.&lt;/strong&gt; Usually it isn't a skilled cracker. It's one buyer sharing their copy with five friends, or a jar reposted on a leak forum the week you launch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can't make piracy impossible, and trying to usually hurts paying customers more than pirates. What you can do is make sharing inconvenient, make leaks traceable, and make paying the easy option. This is the plan I'd follow, in order. Each step is worth doing on its own, so stop wherever the effort stops paying off.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 0: Decide what you're actually fighting
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Threat&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;How common&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What stops it&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A buyer shares their copy or key&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Very common&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Per-buyer keys and machine limits (steps 1-2)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A jar gets reposted on a leak site&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Common&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Per-buyer keys, watermarks, takedowns (steps 1, 6, 8)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Someone deletes your license check&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Less common, easier with AI editors&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Core unlock and obfuscation (steps 4-5)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A determined cracker rewrites your code&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Rare&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Nothing fully. Make it slow and not worth it&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most losses sit in the first two rows, and those are the cheapest to fix.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 1: Give every buyer their own key, automatically
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A single shared password or license file can't tell anyone apart. Give each purchase its own key, so you can see who's using what and switch off exactly one person.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Don't do it by hand. Connect your store so keys are created at checkout. On BuiltByBit, an &lt;em&gt;External license key&lt;/em&gt; placeholder writes the buyer's key into the jar they download. Voxel Shop and SpigotMC have their own placeholders, and Tebex, Stripe, Gumroad, Lemon Squeezy and others can create a key through a webhook. Wire refunds in too, so a refunded purchase loses its key.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 2: Limit where a key can run
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A key that works everywhere is a key that gets shared. Set a limit that fits the product:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Plugins:&lt;/strong&gt; servers per key (1-3 is typical).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Desktop apps:&lt;/strong&gt; machines per key (2-3 covers a laptop and a desktop).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Bots or web tools:&lt;/strong&gt; an IP address limit.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the limit is reached, a new machine is refused with a clear message, and the buyer can free a slot themselves. Be generous: a support ticket costs you more than one extra machine.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F240tm7fmqwk5ys1x4o29.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F240tm7fmqwk5ys1x4o29.png" alt="A policy with Max machines and Max IPs per key" width="800" height="590"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 3: Check at startup, and never take a customer down
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Check the license when the app starts, then again every few hours. No need to phone home every minute. Above all, &lt;strong&gt;only stop on a clear "no"&lt;/strong&gt; (revoked, suspended, expired, too many machines). If your license server can't be reached, keep running. BuiltByBit's DRM guidance says the same, and plugins that disable themselves when a server is down risk being removed. For a Minecraft plugin, I walk through this check line by line in &lt;a href="https://dev.to/ka0/how-to-add-license-keys-to-a-minecraft-plugin-in-10-minutes-bd6"&gt;How to add license keys to a Minecraft plugin in 10 minutes&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For customers without internet, ship a &lt;strong&gt;signed offline license&lt;/strong&gt;: a file signed with a private key (Ed25519, for example) that your app verifies with the public key, and that expires on a date you set.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 4: Make the check hard to delete
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A license check is one &lt;code&gt;if&lt;/code&gt; statement. Anyone with a decompiler, or an AI editor, can delete it. Two things make that pointless:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Core unlock.&lt;/strong&gt; Encrypt a small, essential piece of your code (a core class, default rules, a lookup table) and only decrypt it after a valid license check. Delete the check, and that piece is simply missing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tamper reports.&lt;/strong&gt; Hash your own classes and report a modified copy with the next license check, so you know which license runs a patched build.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 5: Obfuscate your release builds
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rename classes and methods, encrypt strings and scramble control flow, so your check and your core are hard to find and patch. ProGuard gives a safe rename; ProGuard followed by Skidfuscator adds control flow and string encryption. Only obfuscate the build you sell, test it before upload, and keep the mapping file so you can read customers' stack traces. I wrote a step-by-step guide: &lt;a href="https://dev.to/ka0/how-to-obfuscate-a-minecraft-plugin-free-step-by-step-283m"&gt;How to obfuscate a Minecraft plugin&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 6: Watermark every download
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Put something in each buyer's copy that names them: their license key in the jar, an ID in the manifest, a mark in the archive. When the jar shows up on a leak site, you know whose it was without guessing. It also deters sharing: people think twice when their copy carries their name.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 7: Watch for sharing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You won't spot a shared key by reading logs. Get alerts for the patterns that matter: one key on many machines at once, a key checking in from two countries an hour apart, a machine trying random keys, a revoked key still in use, a key pasted in your Discord. Then act on the real ones (a customer who travels isn't a pirate).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fj1dgbjlk0i5skyjapu7i.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fj1dgbjlk0i5skyjapu7i.png" alt="Sentinel watches license checks and flags shared and leaked keys" width="800" height="525"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 8: Respond fast when something leaks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Find the buyer from the watermark or the key in the leaked file.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Revoke that key. Copies stop at their next check; everyone else is untouched.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Send a takedown notice (DMCA) to the site hosting it. Most marketplaces also have a DMCA form.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Revoke refunded and charged-back purchases too, or they keep a free copy.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 9: Make paying easier than pirating
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The best anti-piracy feature is a product that's easy to buy. A fair price, a trial, fast updates, real support and a Discord. A pirate gets an old jar and no help. A customer gets the update that fixes their bug today.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The whole plan on one page
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fmhfwa04htlnlsq7qnosj.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fmhfwa04htlnlsq7qnosj.png" alt="The plan, layer by layer: keys, limits, a safe check, core unlock, obfuscation, watermarks, alerts, response" width="800" height="440"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How to put it together
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can build each layer yourself: a small key server, a signing key, an obfuscation step in your build, and a dashboard to revoke from. Or use a service. I build &lt;a href="https://sunlicense.com/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=guides-2026-11&amp;amp;utm_content=anti-piracy-plan" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SunLicense&lt;/a&gt;, which covers the list:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Step&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;In SunLicense&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Plan&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1. Per-buyer keys from your store&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;BuiltByBit, Voxel Shop, SpigotMC, Tebex, Stripe, Gumroad and more&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2. Machine and IP limits&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Per policy&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3. Safe startup check, offline files&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;SDKs for Java, JavaScript, Python, .NET, PHP and Lua; signed Ed25519 files&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4. Core unlock and tamper reports&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Seal up to 512 KB, 7 days offline grace&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;5. Obfuscation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Maximum, ProGuard, yGuard, Skidfuscator; JavaScript, Python, Lua (FiveM)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free (3 runs a month)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;6. Watermarked downloads&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The buyer's mark in each file&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Standard&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;7. Sharing alerts&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Sentinel detectors, Discord alerts&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;8. Revoke in one click&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Dashboard, CLI, Discord&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Free&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It's free for 3 products and 100 licenses, with no credit card and no cut of your sales. &lt;a href="https://sunlicense.com/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=guides-2026-11&amp;amp;utm_content=anti-piracy-plan-end" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;See what SunLicense covers&lt;/a&gt;, then work through the list, starting with step 1.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Assume your premium plugin will be decompiled the day it goes on sale.&lt;/strong&gt; Anyone can open the jar in a decompiler and read your code like the source, including the license check you spent an evening on.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Obfuscation won't make that impossible. It turns readable code into a puzzle, so copying your logic or patching out a check takes far more work. The catch: a careless obfuscation pass is the fastest way to ship a plugin that doesn't load. Paper and Spigot find your main class by name and call your event handlers by reflection. Rename the wrong thing and the server throws &lt;code&gt;ClassNotFoundException&lt;/code&gt; at startup.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This guide shows two ways to do it safely: the quick way, with a free hosted obfuscator that writes the keep rules for you, and the by-hand way with ProGuard. Then a checklist to test the jar before you sell it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fh6tkfgz1ypf1kk2rrgct.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fh6tkfgz1ypf1kk2rrgct.png" alt="A license check before and after obfuscation, as a decompiler shows it" width="800" height="410"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What obfuscation does, and what it doesn't
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;It does:&lt;/strong&gt; rename your classes, methods and fields to &lt;code&gt;a&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;b&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;c&lt;/code&gt;, encrypt string constants, and scramble control flow. A decompiler still produces Java, but it reads like noise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;It doesn't:&lt;/strong&gt; stop someone determined, or stop a buyer from sharing the jar. It raises the cost of reading and patching your code. For sharing you need a license key per buyer (more at the end). The two work best together: obfuscation hides the check, and the license makes a shared jar useless.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The quick way: free hosted obfuscation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I build &lt;a href="https://sunlicense.com/for/minecraft-plugins?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=guides-2026-11&amp;amp;utm_content=obfuscate-plugin" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SunLicense&lt;/a&gt;, a licensing service for plugin and app sellers. Its free plan includes 3 obfuscation runs a month, which covers a release or two. It runs ProGuard, yGuard and Skidfuscator on its servers, and it reads your jar to find what must keep its name, so you don't write keep rules.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Step 1: Create a free account
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://sunlicense.com/for/minecraft-plugins?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=guides-2026-11&amp;amp;utm_content=obfuscate-plugin-step1" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Sign up&lt;/a&gt; with Google, GitHub, Discord or email. No credit card.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Step 2: Developers → Obfuscation → Java
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open &lt;strong&gt;Obfuscation&lt;/strong&gt; under &lt;strong&gt;Developers&lt;/strong&gt; in the sidebar and pick &lt;strong&gt;Java&lt;/strong&gt;. (The same page handles JavaScript, Python and Lua for FiveM, if you sell those too.)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fspokysh7uvzy1iefe6d0.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fspokysh7uvzy1iefe6d0.png" alt="The Obfuscation page: choose Java, JavaScript/TypeScript, Lua or Python" width="800" height="410"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Step 3: Pick Maximum or ProGuard
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Engine&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What it does&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Pick it when&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Maximum&lt;/strong&gt; (recommended)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;ProGuard renames, then Skidfuscator adds control flow and string encryption&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;You want the strongest protection and will test the result&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ProGuard&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Renames classes, methods and fields&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;You want the safe standard, or Maximum broke something&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;yGuard&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Renames only&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;You need the most compatible option&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Skidfuscator&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Control flow and strings, keeps names&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;You want flow and string protection without renaming&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with &lt;strong&gt;Maximum&lt;/strong&gt;. If your plugin leans heavily on reflection (NMS, Gson models, a command framework) and something misbehaves in testing, run the same jar again with &lt;strong&gt;ProGuard&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F4rtvswlhrulq6ir70nvj.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F4rtvswlhrulq6ir70nvj.png" alt="Choose an obfuscator: Maximum, ProGuard, yGuard and Skidfuscator" width="800" height="530"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Step 4: Upload the built jar
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Build the jar you actually ship (with Shadow, &lt;code&gt;./gradlew shadowJar&lt;/code&gt;) and drop it in. A &lt;code&gt;.jar&lt;/code&gt; or a &lt;code&gt;.zip&lt;/code&gt; of several jars, up to 100 MB, Java up to 25.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SunLicense reads the jar and keeps the names that must survive: the &lt;code&gt;main&lt;/code&gt; class in &lt;code&gt;plugin.yml&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;paper-plugin.yml&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;bungee.yml&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;velocity-plugin.json&lt;/code&gt;, Fabric and Forge entry points and mixins, &lt;code&gt;META-INF/services&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;@EventHandler&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;@Subscribe&lt;/code&gt; methods, enums and serialisable types.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One detail that saves a lot of pain: if you sell on BuiltByBit or Voxel Shop with a download &lt;strong&gt;placeholder&lt;/strong&gt; (a string like &lt;code&gt;%%__SUNLICENSE__%%&lt;/code&gt; the store replaces with the buyer's key), string encryption would hide that string from the store. Classes that hold a placeholder are never string-encrypted, so key injection keeps working.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fsvhqf7nu71ynq00qqowh.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fsvhqf7nu71ynq00qqowh.png" alt="Upload your file: .jar or .zip, up to 100 MB" width="800" height="415"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Optional:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;Advanced options&lt;/strong&gt; has a &lt;strong&gt;Keep unrenamed&lt;/strong&gt; list for extra packages or classes (for example &lt;code&gt;com.example.api.**&lt;/code&gt;), and lets you add library jars you compile against if the report mentions missing classes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Step 5: Run, then download three files
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Choose &lt;strong&gt;Obfuscate&lt;/strong&gt; and watch the live log. It runs as a background task, so you can close the page and come back; results are kept for 7 days and stay on the &lt;strong&gt;History&lt;/strong&gt; tab. You get:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The obfuscated jar&lt;/strong&gt;: the one you upload to your store.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The mapping file&lt;/strong&gt;: keep it for every release. When a buyer sends a stack trace full of &lt;code&gt;a.b.c&lt;/code&gt;, ProGuard's &lt;code&gt;retrace&lt;/code&gt; tool turns it back into your real names.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A short report&lt;/strong&gt; on the run, including any warnings.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Releasing often? The same run works from a release script with the CLI:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;sunlicense obfuscate build/libs/MyPlugin-1.0-all.jar &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--profile&lt;/span&gt; release &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-o&lt;/span&gt; dist/
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


The by-hand way: ProGuard
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you'd rather run it yourself, &lt;a href="https://www.guardsquare.com/proguard" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ProGuard&lt;/a&gt; is the safe choice. What you must keep:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Keep&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Why&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The &lt;code&gt;main:&lt;/code&gt; class in &lt;code&gt;plugin.yml&lt;/code&gt; (and &lt;code&gt;paper-plugin.yml&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;bungee.yml&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;velocity-plugin.json&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The server loads it by exact name&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;@EventHandler&lt;/code&gt; methods&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Found by annotation; shrinking removes "unused" methods&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;ConfigurationSerializable&lt;/code&gt; classes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Saved configs store the class name&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Enum &lt;code&gt;values()&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;valueOf()&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Used when reading config values back&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Anything loaded by reflection&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The name is a string in your code&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A starting config for a shaded jar:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;-injars  build/libs/MyPlugin-1.0-all.jar
-outjars build/obf/MyPlugin-1.0.jar
-libraryjars &amp;lt;java.home&amp;gt;/jmods/java.base.jmod(!**.jar;!module-info.class)
-libraryjars libs/paper-api.jar

# Plugins are loaded by reflection: don't shrink or optimise
-dontshrink
-dontoptimize
-keepattributes *Annotation*,Signature,InnerClasses,EnclosingMethod,SourceFile,LineNumberTable

-keep public class com.example.myplugin.MyPlugin { *; }
-keepclassmembers class * { @org.bukkit.event.EventHandler &amp;lt;methods&amp;gt;; }
-keep class * implements org.bukkit.configuration.serialization.ConfigurationSerializable { *; }
-keepclassmembers enum * {
public static **[] values();
public static ** valueOf(java.lang.String);
}

-printmapping build/obf/mapping.txt
-dontwarn
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Add a &lt;code&gt;-keep&lt;/code&gt; line for every package you reach by reflection. For more strength, run &lt;a href="https://skidfuscator.dev" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Skidfuscator&lt;/a&gt; after ProGuard: turn on string encryption first, test, then control flow, and test again. Exclude the class holding any store placeholder from string encryption.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Test the jar before you sell it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Never upload an obfuscated jar you haven't started. Five minutes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Start a clean Paper server with only your plugin. No errors in the console.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run every command, including tab completion.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Trigger your main events (join, break a block, open your GUI).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Restart the server: config and saved data load back.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Test on every server version listed on your store page.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Open the jar in a decompiler yourself and check it reads like noise.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If something fails, the error names the class. Keep that package unrenamed, or step down from Maximum to ProGuard, and run again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Obfuscation is half the job
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An obfuscated jar that anyone can run is still a jar anyone can share. The other half is &lt;strong&gt;one license key per buyer&lt;/strong&gt;, checked on startup: a shared key then shows up on too many servers, and a leaked jar names its buyer. I wrote that up separately: &lt;a href="https://dev.to/ka0/how-to-add-license-keys-to-a-minecraft-plugin-in-10-minutes-bd6"&gt;How to add license keys to a Minecraft plugin in 10 minutes&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Licensing and obfuscation are both on SunLicense's free plan, with no cut of your sales. &lt;a href="https://sunlicense.com/for/minecraft-plugins?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=guides-2026-11&amp;amp;utm_content=obfuscate-plugin-end" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;See how it works for Minecraft plugins&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


GHSA-47w9-6r3f-938g: Authorization Bypass and Host Root Command Execution in Canonical LXD and Incus
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Vulnerability ID:&lt;/strong&gt; GHSA-47W9-6R3F-938G&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CVSS Score:&lt;/strong&gt; 9.9&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Published:&lt;/strong&gt; 2026-11-06&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A missing authorization and configuration validation vulnerability in Canonical LXD and LXC Incus allows authenticated users within restricted projects to bypass project security boundaries. By smuggling prohibited low-level configuration directives (such as raw.lxc or raw.qemu) inside instance snapshots or backup archives, an attacker can restore these configurations within a restricted project and execute arbitrary commands with root privileges on the underlying host operating system.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


TL;DR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Authenticated users in restricted LXD or Incus projects can bypass security restrictions by restoring instance snapshots or importing backups containing prohibited low-level hypervisor hooks (e.g., raw.lxc), leading to arbitrary command execution as root on the host OS.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


⚠️ Exploit Status: POC
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Technical Details
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Vulnerability ID&lt;/strong&gt;: GHSA-47w9-6r3f-938g / CVE-2026-48751&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Weakness Class&lt;/strong&gt;: CWE-862: Missing Authorization&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Attack Vector&lt;/strong&gt;: Network (REST API)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CVSS v3.1 Score&lt;/strong&gt;: 9.9 Critical (AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:C/C:H/I:H/A:H)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;EPSS Score&lt;/strong&gt;: 0.68% (52.67th Percentile)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Security Impact&lt;/strong&gt;: Container Escape / Host Root Command Execution&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Exploit Status&lt;/strong&gt;: Public Proof-of-Concept Available&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CISA KEV Status&lt;/strong&gt;: Not Listed&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Affected Systems
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Canonical LXD&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;LXC Incus&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;LXD (LTS 4.0 / 5.0 series)&lt;/strong&gt;: &amp;gt;= 4.12.0, &amp;lt; 5.0.7 (Fixed in: &lt;code&gt;5.0.7&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;LXD (LTS 5.21 series)&lt;/strong&gt;: &amp;gt;= 5.1.0, &amp;lt; 5.21.5 (Fixed in: &lt;code&gt;5.21.5&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;LXD (Feature series)&lt;/strong&gt;: &amp;gt;= 6.0.0, &amp;lt; 6.9.0 (Fixed in: &lt;code&gt;6.9.0&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Incus&lt;/strong&gt;: &amp;lt; 7.2.0 (Fixed in: &lt;code&gt;7.2.0&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Code Analysis
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Commit: &lt;a href="https://github.com/canonical/lxd/commit/5aac658c888d52e3a2480a8f879799ac643fdc49" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;5aac658&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Validate instance snapshots against project restrictions when importing from backup in lxd/instances_post.go&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gh"&gt;diff --git a/lxd/instances_post.go b/lxd/instances_post.go
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="gd"&gt;--- a/lxd/instances_post.go
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+++ b/lxd/instances_post.go
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;@@ -932,6 +902,56 @@&lt;/span&gt; func createFromBackup(s *state.State, r *http.Request, projectName string, data
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+   // Check restrictions/limits if defined on project.
+   if restrictions != nil {
+       err = limits.AllowInstanceCreation(s.GlobalConfig, *restrictions, req)
+       if err != nil {
+           return response.SmartError(err)
+       }
+
+       for i, snapshot := range bInfo.Config.Snapshots {
+           if snapshot == nil {
+               return response.SmartError(fmt.Errorf("Nil instance snapshot definition found at index %d", i))
+           }
+
+           snapshotReq := api.InstancesPost{
+               InstancePut: api.InstancePut{
+                   Architecture: snapshot.Architecture,
+                   Config:       snapshot.Config,
+                   Devices:      snapshot.Devices,
+                   Ephemeral:    snapshot.Ephemeral,
+                   Profiles:     snapshot.Profiles,
+                   Stateful:     snapshot.Stateful,
+               },
+               Name:   bInfo.Name + "/" + snapshot.Name,
+               Source: api.InstanceSource{},
+               Type:   api.InstanceType(bInfo.Config.Instance.Type),
+           }
+
+           err = limits.AllowInstanceCreation(s.GlobalConfig, *restrictions, snapshotReq)
+           if err != nil {
+               return response.SmartError(err)
+           }
+       }
+   }
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


Commit: &lt;a href="https://github.com/canonical/lxd/commit/a52e5d06a52fb7ad8862cb2a8659a8c47e446efe" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;a52e5d0&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Validate snapshot config against project limits and restrictions during snapshot restore in lxd/instance_put.go (master)&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gh"&gt;diff --git a/lxd/instance_put.go b/lxd/instance_put.go
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="gd"&gt;--- a/lxd/instance_put.go
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+++ b/lxd/instance_put.go
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;@@ -235,6 +235,32 @@&lt;/span&gt; func instanceSnapRestore(s *state.State, projectName string, name string, snap s
}
}
&lt;span class="err"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+   // Build the instance config that restoring the snapshot would apply, so it can be
+   // validated against the project's restrictions and limits below.
+   snapProfileNames := make([]string, 0, len(source.Profiles()))
+   for _, profile := range source.Profiles() {
+       snapProfileNames = append(snapProfileNames, profile.Name)
+   }
+
+   snapConfig := api.InstancePut{
+       Config:   source.LocalConfig(),
+       Devices:  source.LocalDevices().CloneNative(),
+       Profiles: snapProfileNames,
+   }
+
+   err = s.DB.Cluster.Transaction(s.ShutdownCtx, func(ctx context.Context, tx *db.ClusterTx) error {
+       return limits.AllowInstanceUpdate(ctx, s.GlobalConfig, tx, projectName, name, snapConfig, inst.LocalConfig())
+   })
+   if err != nil {
+       return err
+   }
+
&lt;/span&gt;    // Generate a new `volatile.uuid.generation` to differentiate this instance restored from a snapshot from the original instance.
source.LocalConfig()["volatile.uuid.generation"] = uuid.New().String()
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


Commit: &lt;a href="https://github.com/canonical/lxd/commit/6b93b2a0c4f6f3eaf91e1e813f6875d5eb66b59b" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;6b93b2a&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Backport of snapshot restore project restriction validation to LXD 5.0 branch&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Commit: &lt;a href="https://github.com/canonical/lxd/commit/cfd9f66f6af89e1fa3b3acb12bfe72f51b7f1e19" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;cfd9f66&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Backport of snapshot restore project restriction validation to LXD 5.21 branch&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Exploit Details
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://github.com/canonical/lxd/security/advisories/GHSA-47w9-6r3f-938g" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub Security Advisory&lt;/a&gt;: Official advisory containing the proof-of-concept CLI commands to bypass project restrictions via snapshot restoration.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://github.com/lxc/incus/security/advisories/GHSA-48q5-w887-33wv" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub Security Advisory (Incus)&lt;/a&gt;: Upstream Incus security advisory detailing the reproduction steps using incus CLI.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Mitigation Strategies
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Upgrade Canonical LXD or LXC Incus to a patched version that enforces project restriction checks during snapshot restoration and backup ingestion.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Audit all existing instances and snapshots in restricted projects for unauthorized low-level configuration keys such as raw.lxc and raw.qemu.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Restrict untrusted users from migrating instances across projects or importing custom backup tarballs until patches are applied.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Remediation Steps:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For Canonical LXD 5.0 LTS environments, upgrade the snap or package to version 5.0.7 or later.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For Canonical LXD 5.21 LTS environments, upgrade the snap or package to version 5.21.5 or later.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For Canonical LXD 6.x environments, upgrade to version 6.9.0 or later.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For LXC Incus environments, upgrade the incus package to version 7.2.0 or later.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run an API audit across all restricted projects using 'lxc query /1.0/instances?recursion=2' (or 'incus query') to identify and delete any snapshots or active configurations containing prohibited raw.* directives.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/canonical/lxd/security/advisories/GHSA-47w9-6r3f-938g" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub Advisory GHSA-47w9-6r3f-938g (Canonical LXD)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/lxc/incus/security/advisories/GHSA-48q5-w887-33wv" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub Advisory GHSA-48q5-w887-33wv (LXC Incus)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-48751" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;National Vulnerability Database (NVD) - CVE-2026-48751&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2026-48751" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Official CVE.org Record - CVE-2026-48751&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/canonical/lxd/commit/5aac658c888d52e3a2480a8f879799ac643fdc49" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Canonical LXD Fix Commit: Backup Snapshot Validation (5aac658c)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/canonical/lxd/commit/a52e5d06a52fb7ad8862cb2a8659a8c47e446efe" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Canonical LXD Fix Commit: Snapshot Restore Validation Master (a52e5d06)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/canonical/lxd/commit/6b93b2a0c4f6f3eaf91e1e813f6875d5eb66b59b" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Canonical LXD Fix Commit: Snapshot Restore Validation 5.0 (6b93b2a0)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/canonical/lxd/commit/cfd9f66f6af89e1fa3b3acb12bfe72f51b7f1e19" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Canonical LXD Fix Commit: Snapshot Restore Validation 5.21 (cfd9f66f)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/canonical/lxd/releases/tag/lxd-5.0.7" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Canonical LXD Release 5.0.7&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/canonical/lxd/releases/tag/lxd-5.21.5" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Canonical LXD Release 5.21.5&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;a href="https://cvereports.com/reports/GHSA-47W9-6R3F-938G" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Read the full report for GHSA-47W9-6R3F-938G on our website&lt;/a&gt; for more details including interactive diagrams and full exploit analysis.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Yes. Multifactor authentication is requirement 03.05.03, one of the 13 CPCSC Level 1 requirements. PSPC’s how-to page says it is required for privileged accounts and for systems that store Specified Information, and I would plan for every account that touches those systems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The reasoning
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;PSPC’s overview lists MFA as control 7 of the Level 1 set, under identification and authentication. Its how-to page words the duty as MFA for privileged accounts and for systems that store Specified Information. ITSP.10.171 and PSPC’s assessment criteria cover privileged and non-privileged accounts, which is the wider reading.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I plan for the wider reading. The criteria are what you sign against when you attest, and a gap on ordinary user accounts that can open the drawings is hard to defend. MFA is cheap to turn on and expensive to explain away.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Shops coming from CMMC are often surprised by this. The American Level 1 has no MFA requirement, so passing it on passwords alone says nothing about CPCSC Level 1.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Edge cases
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Which systems are covered depends on your boundary. If Specified Information lives in an enclave file share plus email, MFA covers those logins and the admin accounts that manage them. Draw the boundary first, using the &lt;a href="https://hans.study/cpcsc/scoping/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;scoping page&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Shared logins are a problem. MFA tied to a shared shop-floor account doesn’t identify anyone, and requirement 03.05.01 asks for unique identification.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On method, authenticator apps work and hardware keys are the option I push. SMS is the weakest choice, and I would avoid it for admin accounts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Evidence is simple. PSPC’s list of examples includes screenshots of MFA configuration, so a dated screenshot per system is enough to start.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Remote access deserves its own look. A VPN or remote desktop gateway that reaches the enclave is a privileged path in practice, even when the account behind it is an ordinary one. If MFA is on the Microsoft 365 sign-in but not on the VPN, the front door is locked and the side door isn’t. Fix both before you attest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The cost of getting this wrong is mostly an attestation problem. Level 1 has no routine evidence review, and PSPC reserves the right to look, so the signature is what carries the weight. I would rather a shop delay its attestation by two weeks to turn MFA on than sign and hope.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What to do this week
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt; List every system that stores or can reach Specified Information, including email and remote access.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; List every privileged account on them, and then every ordinary user account.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; Turn on MFA for the admin accounts first, then everyone else.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt; Capture a dated screenshot of each configuration and file it under 03.05.03.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://hans.study/cpcsc/level-1/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Level 1 page&lt;/a&gt; has the other 12 requirements, and &lt;a href="https://hans.study/cpcsc-bytes/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;CPCSC Bytes&lt;/a&gt; has short notes on the rest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To check MFA coverage against your own boundary, &lt;a href="https://hans.study/book/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;book a scoping call&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Selling a premium plugin without license keys?&lt;/strong&gt; Then every copy is the same jar. One buyer can hand it to a whole network, and when it turns up on a leak site you can't tell whose copy it was.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This tutorial adds licensing to a Paper plugin in about ten minutes. Every buyer gets their own key automatically when they download from BuiltByBit, a key works on a limited number of servers, and you can switch off one leaked copy without touching anyone else. It follows BuiltByBit's DRM guidance, so it never takes a paying customer's server down.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I build &lt;a href="https://sunlicense.com/for/minecraft-plugins?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=guides-2026-11&amp;amp;utm_content=license-keys-plugin" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SunLicense&lt;/a&gt;, and I'll use it as the license server here (the free plan covers 3 products). The design works the same with any licensing service.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The design, in four rules
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;One key per buyer&lt;/strong&gt;, written into the jar by the store. No keys to send by hand.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A server limit per key&lt;/strong&gt;, for example two servers per purchase.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Check on startup, re-check every few hours.&lt;/strong&gt; No phoning home every minute.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Only stop on a clear "no".&lt;/strong&gt; If the license server can't be reached, keep running.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rule 4 matters most. BuiltByBit's DRM guidance warns that a plugin which disables itself whenever a server is unreachable can be treated as malicious and removed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fr52u91pogsfcamhbkzx9.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fr52u91pogsfcamhbkzx9.png" alt="One check, three answers: valid starts the plugin, a clear no disables it, unreachable keeps running" width="800" height="320"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 1: Create the product and a policy (2 minutes)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://sunlicense.com/for/minecraft-plugins?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=guides-2026-11&amp;amp;utm_content=license-keys-plugin-step1" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Create a free account&lt;/a&gt; (Google, GitHub, Discord or email, no credit card), then:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Products → New product&lt;/strong&gt;, for example "MyPlugin".&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;New policy&lt;/strong&gt;: this is what one purchase gets. Set &lt;strong&gt;Max machines&lt;/strong&gt; to 2. For a plugin, a machine is a server.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;API keys → New key&lt;/strong&gt;. Your plugin uses it to check licenses. Keep it out of git.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ft6nymhutbfydfbv0xqkn.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Ft6nymhutbfydfbv0xqkn.png" alt="The New policy dialog with Max machines set to 2" width="800" height="590"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 2: Add the check (5 minutes)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The fastest way:&lt;/strong&gt; start from the free &lt;a href="https://github.com/sunlicense-com/example-paper-plugin" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Paper plugin template&lt;/a&gt;. It already has the SDK relocated into your package with Shadow, a console message for every result, the re-check every few hours, an offline-license fallback, and it keeps running when the server can't be reached.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Click &lt;strong&gt;Use this template&lt;/strong&gt; on GitHub, then rename &lt;code&gt;com.example.myplugin&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Put the API key in &lt;code&gt;~/.gradle/gradle.properties&lt;/code&gt; as &lt;code&gt;sunlicenseApiKey=slk_...&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run &lt;code&gt;./gradlew runServer&lt;/code&gt; to start a local Paper server with your plugin.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Put your features in &lt;code&gt;core/CoreFeatures.java&lt;/code&gt;. They start once the license is valid.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Adding it to an existing plugin instead?&lt;/strong&gt; Add the Java SDK (&lt;code&gt;com.sunlicense:sunlicense&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;a href="https://docs.sunlicense.com/guide/sdks" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;current version in the docs&lt;/a&gt;), shade and relocate it, and run the check off the main thread:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight java"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// BuiltByBit replaces this string with the buyer's key when they download.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;static&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;final&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;String&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;LICENSE_KEY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;"%%__SUNLICENSE__%%"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;SunLicense&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;String&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;serverId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@Override&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;onEnable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;SunLicense&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;Builder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;baseUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"https://api.sunlicense.com"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;token&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;API_KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;timeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;ofSeconds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;build&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;serverId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;serverId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;IOException&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;serverId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;"unknown"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Check now, then every 6 hours, on a background thread.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kt"&gt;long&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sixHours&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;20L&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;60&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;60&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;6&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;getServer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;getScheduler&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;runTaskTimerAsynchronously&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;checkLicense&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0L&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sixHours&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ...start your plugin&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;checkLicense&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;SunLicense&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;Result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;client&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;validate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;LICENSE_KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"host"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;serverId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;isValid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;getStatus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;400&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// A clear "no": revoked, suspended, expired or too many servers.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;getLogger&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;severe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"This copy is not licensed. Contact the author."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;getServer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;getScheduler&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;runTask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;getServer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;getPluginManager&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;disablePlugin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// A server error is not a "no".&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;getLogger&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;warning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"License server answered "&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;getStatus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;", running anyway."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;SunLicense&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;SunLicenseException&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Can't reach the license server: keep running, check again later.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;getLogger&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;warning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"License server unreachable, running anyway."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// A random id saved in the plugin folder, so each server counts once.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;String&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;serverId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;throws&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;IOException&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Path&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;file&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;getDataFolder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;toPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;".server-id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Files&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;isRegularFile&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;file&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Files&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;readString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;file&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;trim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;String&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;UUID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;randomUUID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;toString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Files&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;createDirectories&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;file&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;getParent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;());&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Files&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;writeString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;file&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="o"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Two details worth copying: only HTTP 400 counts as a "no" (a 5xx or a timeout never stops a server), and the server id is a stable random id rather than the IP, which is often blank on hosted servers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 3: Let BuiltByBit deliver the keys (2 minutes)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;In SunLicense: &lt;strong&gt;Integrations → BuiltByBit → Connect&lt;/strong&gt;. Map your resource ID (the number at the end of your resource URL) to the policy. SunLicense creates the secret and shows the hook URL.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;On BuiltByBit: &lt;strong&gt;Creator dashboard → Placeholders → Create placeholder&lt;/strong&gt;:

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Placeholder: &lt;code&gt;%%__SUNLICENSE__%%&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Type: &lt;strong&gt;External license key&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;URL and secret: from step 1&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Apply it to your resource and &lt;strong&gt;publish an update&lt;/strong&gt;. Placeholders only apply to files uploaded after they exist.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fr1oj8cymzm4dwavtxs1w.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fr1oj8cymzm4dwavtxs1w.png" alt="The Connect BuiltByBit dialog: resource ID mapped to a policy, secret created for you" width="800" height="525"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now each download contains that buyer's own key, the same one every time they re-download. Selling on Voxel Shop or SpigotMC instead? Both have their own placeholder integration on the same page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 4: Test it like a buyer (1 minute)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Download your resource from BuiltByBit once and check the jar contains a key, not the placeholder string.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Start a server: the console says the license is valid.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Start a third server with the same key: it's refused (too many servers).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Revoke the license in the dashboard: at the next check, the plugin stops with your message.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Block the license server (for example in your hosts file) and restart: the plugin keeps running.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What you get
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A shared key gets refused&lt;/strong&gt; past the server limit.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A leaked jar names its buyer.&lt;/strong&gt; The license is named after their BuiltByBit user ID, so you revoke that one license and every copy of that jar stops at its next check.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Paying customers never notice:&lt;/strong&gt; one check on startup, a quiet re-check, and it keeps running if your license server has a bad day.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fz2eddueqsp4i21ro5cd0.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fz2eddueqsp4i21ro5cd0.png" alt="The licenses list: one masked key per buyer, each with its own status" width="800" height="565"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Going further
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Offline licenses:&lt;/strong&gt; a signed file for servers without internet access.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Seal a core:&lt;/strong&gt; with the template, &lt;code&gt;./gradlew build -PsunlicenseProduct=&amp;lt;code&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; seals a part of your plugin so only a valid license unseals it. Deleting the check then doesn't help a pirate.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Obfuscate the release jar:&lt;/strong&gt; see &lt;a href="https://dev.to/ka0/how-to-obfuscate-a-minecraft-plugin-free-step-by-step-283m"&gt;How to obfuscate a Minecraft plugin&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Let your coding agent do it:&lt;/strong&gt; the product's &lt;strong&gt;Setup&lt;/strong&gt; tab has a prompt for Claude Code, Cursor or Codex that adds all of this to your project and proves it works with a test license.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Licensing, the BuiltByBit integration and obfuscation are all on SunLicense's free plan, with no cut of your sales. &lt;a href="https://sunlicense.com/for/minecraft-plugins?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=article&amp;amp;utm_campaign=guides-2026-11&amp;amp;utm_content=license-keys-plugin-end" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;See how it works for Minecraft plugins&lt;/a&gt; and protect your next release.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Cloudflare D1's free tier allows &lt;strong&gt;100,000 rows written and 5 million rows read a day&lt;/strong&gt;, counted across every database on the account, and resets at &lt;strong&gt;00:00 UTC&lt;/strong&gt;. When you go over, every query on every D1 database in the account fails until the reset. We found that out by taking three products down with one load test. Here is what the limits are, what counts as a row, and how to stay under them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The limits on Workers Free
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Limit&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Workers Free&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Workers Paid&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Rows read&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;5 million a day&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;25 billion a month included&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Rows written&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;100,000 a day&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;50 million a month included&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Storage&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;5 GB for the account&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;5 GB included, up to 1 TB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Database size&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;500 MB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;10 GB&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Databases&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;10&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;100,000 included&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Queries per Worker invocation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;50&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1,000&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From Cloudflare's D1 pricing and limits pages, checked on 6 November 2026. Workers Paid is 5 US dollars a month. A &lt;code&gt;db.batch()&lt;/code&gt; call counts as one query toward the per-invocation limit, but every row inside it still counts toward reads and writes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What counts as a row written
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is where the 100,000 goes faster than you expect.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Every inserted, updated or deleted row&lt;/strong&gt; counts. Inserting 10 rows is 10.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Every index the write touches&lt;/strong&gt; adds a row. A table with two indexes on the columns you insert costs three rows per insert.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Deletes count.&lt;/strong&gt; Cleaning up after a test costs as much as the test did, and a retention job that deletes old rows every night spends writes every night.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Schema changes&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;CREATE&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;ALTER&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;DROP&lt;/code&gt;) can count as reads and writes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So one logical event is usually several rows. An email OTP in SignOps is about 8 over its life: the code row and its index, a daily counter, the check, then the deletion when it expires. At 8 rows each, 100,000 writes is about 12,000 codes a day, before anything else on the account writes a row.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What happened to us
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We run three products on one Cloudflare account: SignOps, &lt;a href="https://triplli.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Triplli&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://triplli.com/travel-quotation-software/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;quotation software for travel agencies&lt;/a&gt; in India, and &lt;a href="https://cabarobytriplli.triplli.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Cabaro&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://cabarobytriplli.triplli.com/taxi-booking-software/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;taxi booking software&lt;/a&gt; for cab operators. Each has its own D1 database. On the free plan they share one daily allowance.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On 20 October 2026 we load tested SignOps push on production: 26,000 imported device tokens, three sends to 25,000 devices, then deleting the test devices. Each failed send wrote a row per device, and the cleanup wrote the same again. The account went past 100,000 rows written, and from then until 00:00 UTC, every write failed on all three products, including a travel agency quoting live customers in Triplli. Reads kept working, so pages loaded and every save failed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The only fix that day was to wait.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How to stay under the limit
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Work out the row cost before a test.&lt;/strong&gt; Rows written times (1 + indexes touched), plus the cleanup. 26,000 devices with two indexes is 78,000 rows to insert and 78,000 to delete: over the daily limit before a single send.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Run volume tests locally.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;wrangler dev&lt;/code&gt; with a local D1 (&lt;code&gt;wrangler d1 migrations apply DB --local&lt;/code&gt;) has no daily limit. Keep production tests to a few hundred rows.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Store counts, not events.&lt;/strong&gt; We replaced one row per failed device with one row per message, platform and reason, holding a count. A 25,000-device send with every token failing now writes a handful of rows instead of 25,000.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Cap per customer, per day.&lt;/strong&gt; If customers' usage writes rows, give each organisation a daily ceiling well under the account's allowance, so one busy customer cannot use it all.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Give each product its own account.&lt;/strong&gt; The limit is per account. A second free Cloudflare account gives a second product its own 100,000 writes, and a spike in one cannot reach the other. It costs nothing but a login.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Or pay 5 dollars.&lt;/strong&gt; Workers Paid includes 50 million rows written a month, about 1.6 million a day, and lifts the daily cutoff within minutes of upgrading.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Checking how close you are
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Cloudflare dashboard shows rows read and written per database under Storage and Databases, D1, Metrics. For the account total, add up every database, or query the GraphQL Analytics API's &lt;code&gt;d1AnalyticsAdaptiveGroups&lt;/code&gt; dataset for &lt;code&gt;rowsWritten&lt;/code&gt; grouped by day. Check it after any change that adds a table, an index or a scheduled job.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;First published on the &lt;a href="https://signops.dev/blog/cloudflare-d1-free-tier-limits/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SignOps blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your agent "forgets" between sessions, drifts on stale exports, or eats the context window on raw API payloads. Often the fix is memory and context design rather than a model upgrade. Below is one practical takeaway per episode from Chain of Thought's collection on how agents remember, in the order I'd revisit them when hardening production memory.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why treat memory as its own discipline?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In &lt;a href="https://chainofthought.show/podcast/53-agent-memory-the-last-battleground-in-the-ai-stack-richmond-alake-oracle/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Agent Memory: The Last Battleground in the AI Stack&lt;/a&gt;, Richmond Alake makes memory engineering a named practice next to prompt engineering and context engineering, with its own failure modes. Two he returns to often: the wrong mental model, and deleting records when you mean to forget them. His line for production is simple: don't delete, forget. Controlled decay, using the Generative Agents paper's mix of relevance, recency, and importance, is how he talks about implementing that.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;He maps working, episodic, semantic, and procedural memory onto agent architecture, and segments what enters the window by type instead of one flat transcript. He demos converged search (vector, graph, spatial, relational) in a memory-aware financial services agent. Files work for prototypes; databases are what he points to when memory has to carry load. If you take one rule from the episode, retire or down-rank memories rather than hard-deleting because a screen got cluttered.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A sketch of the scoring idea (illustration only):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;memory_score&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;relevance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;recency&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;importance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;relevance&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;recency&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;importance&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;should_surface&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;score&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;threshold&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;bool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;score&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;threshold&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# forget = below threshold, not DELETE
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


What breaks when the context feeding the agent is wrong?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Michel Tricot's thesis in &lt;a href="https://chainofthought.show/podcast/52-context-poisoning-is-killing-your-ai-agents-how-to-stop-it/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Context Poisoning is Killing Your AI Agents: How to Stop it&lt;/a&gt; is that agents fail because the data reaching them is wrong, rather than because the model is bad. He demos a Gong query through raw API calls that burned 30,000 extra tokens and took about three minutes. The same query through a context store ran in about a minute and used a fraction of the context window.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Treat that as a pre-flight check before you add retrieval tricks: log what actually lands in context. Poisoned or bloated context reads as bad reasoning. Put a curated context layer in front of chatty integrations, especially when you join entities across Salesforce, Zendesk, and Gong. He also describes memory as another form of context; I would fix upstream context before anything fancier in memory.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What compounds if agent loops get commoditized?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Jerry Liu's &lt;a href="https://chainofthought.show/podcast/61-the-ai-framework-era-is-over-why-context-is-the-moat-jerry-liu-of-llamaindex/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The AI Framework Era Is Over: Why Context Is the Moat&lt;/a&gt; is a wager on what survives when harnesses absorb yesterday's scaffolding: context quality. He built LlamaIndex into widely installed plumbing, then argued the framework era is over while the context layer compounds.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On agent memory, he frames it as persistent context: what should still be available after the agent loop ends. If you are allocating engineering time, I'd overweight document and context fidelity (he names 95%+ accuracy as the bar for legal, insurance, and financial document work) over another orchestration wrapper. Persistent memory is only as trustworthy as the context you stored and can rehydrate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What has to change when state outlives one conversation?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://chainofthought.show/podcast/33-mastering-multi-agent-systems-mongodbs-mikiko-chandrasekhar/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Mikiko Chandrasekhar on mastering multi-agent systems&lt;/a&gt; lays out MongoDB's pitch to be the memory store for agents once their state outlives the conversation. The data layer takes on vector search, durable storage, and debugging chat logs as multiple agents share state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Her builder takeaway is to treat agents like software products. Reliability for non-deterministic systems still means observability, evaluations, and guardrails. When memory is shared across agents, the store is part of the product: version writes, log retrievals, and give operators a path from a bad answer to a bad memory write.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How do you stop bolting memory onto the side?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Aishwarya Srinivasan's &lt;a href="https://chainofthought.show/podcast/42-architecting-ai-agents-the-shift-from-models-to-systems-aishwarya-srinivasan/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Architecting AI Agents: The Shift from Models to Systems&lt;/a&gt; argues most agents are built backwards, starting with models instead of system architecture. She walks prompt engineering into context engineering, and says production agents need memory orchestrated alongside models and tool calls rather than bolted on later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Benchmarks alone will not carry you; she pushes evaluation-driven development and human-in-the-loop limits on autonomy. On a whiteboard, put memory on the same diagram as tools and model routes. If memory is a late add-on box, new tools risk fighting your orchestration layer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Name memory engineering explicitly: segment by memory type, and prefer forget-with-decay over delete in production (Richmond Alake, episode 53).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Measure what enters the window before blaming the model; cut context poisoning and token waste with a store in front of noisy APIs (Michel Tricot, episode 52).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Treat persistent agent memory as high-quality context that compounds, and hold document pipelines to the accuracy bar your vertical needs (Jerry Liu, episode 61).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Design the data layer for shared, long-lived agent state, retrieval, and traceable chat history when chats end but work continues (Mikiko Chandrasekhar, episode 33).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Orchestrate memory with models and tools in one architecture, and ship evals with the agent instead of after it (Aishwarya Srinivasan, episode 42).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;More on this topic, with the related episodes, is on &lt;a href="https://chainofthought.show/collections/best-on-agent-memory/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Chain of Thought&lt;/a&gt;. It draws on &lt;a href="https://chainofthought.show/podcast/53-agent-memory-the-last-battleground-in-the-ai-stack-richmond-alake-oracle/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;this episode&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;  &lt;iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/CDadnSE7Eww" width="710" height="399"&gt;
&lt;/iframe&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://newsletter.chainofthought.show/subscribe?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=newsletter" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Subscribe to the Chain of Thought newsletter&lt;/a&gt; for new episodes and write-ups like this one.&lt;/p&gt;

