&lt;p&gt;Building multi-agent systems often sounds great until you actually try running sequential tasks. You start with a clear architecture spec, and by step four, your LLM has forgotten half its constraints.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To fix this, I stopped using monolithic system prompts and built an open-source framework of &lt;strong&gt;64 specialized agent roles&lt;/strong&gt; tied to strict execution runbooks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The Problem: Context Degradation Across Handoffs
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When an agent acts as a generalist (handling product specs, architecture, AND code writing simultaneously), the attention mechanism dilutes key constraints. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The Solution: Isolated Agent Schemas
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;By splitting tasks into hyper-focused roles—such as an &lt;em&gt;API Contract Validator&lt;/em&gt; or an &lt;em&gt;Edge-Case Test Engineer&lt;/em&gt;—we can pass structured JSON handoffs between distinct system prompts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is a simplified example of how we isolate context boundaries in Python/CLI:&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;
bash
# Example invocation of an isolated agent role
npx ai-product-team execute --role architecture-validator --input ./spec.json

### Try It Out &amp;amp; Contribute
The entire project is open-source. You can inspect the complete set of 64 agent personas, runbooks, and CLI integrations directly on GitHub:

&lt;div class="ltag-github-readme-tag"&gt;
